projects.description:
UN Women will support the government to coordinate, implement and monitor the commitments in the JONAP 1325:
•	Integrate a gender perspective in the prevention of violence extremism;
•	Gender responsive security sector reform;
•	Improve access to justice particularly for women and girl’s victims of violence;
•	Building civil society initiatives for peace and security.

The support to the government includes:
- Integrating a gender perspective in the prevention of violence extremism;
- Reforming gender responsive security sector;
- Improving access to justice particularly for women and girl’s victims of violence;
- Building civil society initiatives for peace and security.

UNWomen will manage a pooled fund mechanism with contributions from the UK, Canada, Finland, Norway and Spain.
Implementation is with many stakeholders including GoJ, NGOs, CSOs, Academia, Women Organizations

 The interventions focus on: 
- Supporting MoE in institutionalizing coherent crisis and disaster management approach; 
- Supporting the MoE in the establishment of a crisis and risk management unit;
- Strengthening Data and information production and use for improved crisis sensitive planning;
- Provision of technical support to SDGs (in particular SDG4 and SDG5).

 •	Facilitate evidence-based policy dialogue on the impact of investments on barriers to women's employment for economic growth; 
•	Support social mobilization and advocacy on key challenges to women’s labour force participation, particularly harmful social norms; 
•	Provide integrated policy advice and technical assistance to government on legislation, policies and strategies for women’s improved access to income security and decent work;
•	Support innovative partnerships and platforms with private sector, civil society and government for delivering trainings for women in non-traditional technical and vocational sectors, as well as building their entrepreneurship skills with a focus on challenging gender stereotypes around women and men’s responsibilities concerning unpaid care and household work.

The intervention aims to:
- Facilitate evidence-based policy dialogue on the impact of investments on barriers to women's employment for economic growth; 
- Support social mobilization and advocacy on key challenges to women’s labour force participation, particularly harmful social norms; 
- Provide integrated policy advice and technical assistance to government on legislation, policies and strategies for women’s improved access to income security and decent work;
- Support innovative partnerships and platforms with private sector, civil society and government for delivering trainings for women in non-traditional technical and vocational sectors, as well as building their entrepreneurship skills with a focus on challenging gender stereotypes around women and men’s responsibilities concerning unpaid care and household work.

 - The Oasis model is based on three interlinked pillars: 
(i) Access secure livelihoods opportunities;
(ii) GBV prevention, protection and awareness raising services;
(iii) Education, leadership and civic engagement initiatives, which also targets men and boys.

The intervention focuses on:
- The provision of quality health services to migrant workers (including medical assessment; 
- Facilitating access to testing for COVID-19; 
- Primary health care; referral to secondary and tertiary health facilities;
- Capacity building for migrants  to identify and respond to their health needs in light of the COVID-19 crisis;
- Provision of equipment and supplies to partner health facilities;
 - Referral to legal aid and protection for migrants;
- Assisted Voluntary Return to stranded migrants and victims of trafficking; 
- Awareness raising on migrants vulnerabilities and stigma prevention.

The proposed intervention would:
- Support national institutional capacity to collect and analyze gender statistics to inform policy making, advocacy and accountability for delivering Gender Empowerment and Women Empowerment; 
- Strengthen institutional and financial environment, production, accessibility and use of gender statistics to ensure the effective monitoring of the GE commitments in national plans and in the 2030 agenda.

The intervention aims at supporting social sector reforms and the development of an equitable and inclusive society in Jordan through: 
-Enhancing the physical environment of the 100 social care centers to comply with the international standards of access to social protection services, thereby, allowing the Ministry of Social Development (MoSD) to implement a new sector policy with the aim to provide quality services and to comply with international standards; 
-Strengthening the capacity of MoSD through the development of a Monitoring and Evaluation-based performance Management Information System, and the provision of IT equipment for the National Aid Fund (NAF).
